      "problem": "Find the root of $f(x) = 3(2)^{x} - 48.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the root of $f(x),$  we must solve $f(x)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n3(2)^{x} - 48 = 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we isolate the term with the variable exponent by adding $48$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)^{x}-48 & =0 \\\\ 3(2)^{x}-\\cancel{48}+\\cancel{48} & =0+48 \\\\ 3(2)^{x} & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2)^{x} & =48 \\\\ {2}^{x} & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite $16$ as $2^4$ to get\n\n$$\n2^x = 2^4.\n$$\n\nFinally, since both sides of the equation share the same base $(2)$, we equate the exponents. This gives\n\n$$\nx = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the root of $f(x)$ is $x=4.$",
